<!-- this preview was written by Chrissy Safranski after the 2024 Doenet workshop -->

<setup>
  <function name="f">sin(x)</function>
  <function name="fP">cos(x)</function>
  <function name="fPP">-sin(x)</function>
  <function name="fPPP">-cos(x)</function>

  <function name="T3">$b0 + $b1*x + $b2*x^2 + $b3*x^3</function>
  <function name="T1" styleNumber="2">$b0 + $b1*x</function>


  <function name="T2">$b0 + $b1*x + $b2*x^2</function>
  <function name="T2P">$b1 + 2*$b2*x</function>
  <function name="T2PP">2*$b2</function>
  <function name="T2Symb">b_0 + b_1*x + b_2*x^2</function>
  <function name="T2PSymb">b_1 + 2*b_2*x</function>
  <function name="T2PPSymb">2b_2</function>

  <math name="a">0</math>
  <math name="b0">$$f($a)</math>
  <math name="b1">$$fP($a)</math>
  <math name="b2">$$fPP($a)/2</math>
  <math name="b3">$$fPPP($a)/6</math>
</setup>
      <p>
        Let <m>f(x) = \sin(x)</m> and let <m>T_3(x) = c_0 + c_1 x + c_2 x^2 + c_3x^3</m>.  Our goal is to find the values of <m>c_0, \ldots, c_3</m> that make the sine function and its derivative values agree with those of the cubic polynomial <m>T_3</m> at <m>a = 0</m> and to study the resulting degree <m>3</m> approximation of the sine function.
      </p>
<p>
  <ol marker="a.">
    <li>
      <p>
        As in previous work, the derivatives of <m>T_3(x)</m> and their respective values at <m>a = 0</m> are those shown below.
      </p>
      <p>
            Compute the first three derivatives of <m>f(x) = \sin(x)</m> and evaluate them at <m>a = 0</m> accordingly, recording your results in the blanks provided below.
      </p>

      <p>
        Complete the table below by computing derivatives of <m>f(x)</m> and <m>T_2(x)</m> and evaluating them at <m>x = 0</m> as directed.  Initially, several of your responses will involve the unknown coefficients <m>b_0</m>, <m>b_1</m>, and <m>b_2</m>.  Where needed, use underscores to enter the coefficients. For example, to enter <m>b_1</m> type <q>b_1</q>.
      </p>
              <tabular>
                <row>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>f(x) = </m></cell>
                  <cell><m>$f</m></cell>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>T_3(x) = </m></cell>
                  <cell><m>c_0 + c_1x + c_2x^2 + c_3 x^3</m></cell>
                </row>
               <row>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>f'(x) = </m></cell>
                  <cell><answer name="f1">$fP</answer></cell>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>T_3'(x) = </m></cell>
                  <cell><m>c_1 + 2 c_2 x + 3 c_3 x^2</m></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>f''(x) = </m></cell>
                  <cell><answer name="f2">$fPP</answer></cell>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>T_3''(x) = </m></cell>
                  <cell><m>2 c_2 + 6 c_3 x</m></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>f'''(x) = </m></cell>
                  <cell><answer name="f2">$fPPP</answer></cell>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>T_3'''(x) = </m></cell>
                  <cell><m>6 c_3</m></cell>
                </row>
              </tabular>

              <tabular>
                <row>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>f($a) = </m></cell>
                  <cell><answer name="fa">$$f($a)</answer></cell>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>T_3($a) = </m></cell>
                  <cell><m>c_0</m></cell>
                </row>
               <row>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>f'($a) = </m></cell>
                  <cell><answer name="f1a">$$fP($a)</answer></cell>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>T_3'($a) = </m></cell>
                  <cell><m>c_1</m></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>f''($a) = </m></cell>
                  <cell><answer name="f2a">$$fPP($a)</answer></cell>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>T_3''($a) = </m></cell>
                  <cell><m>2c_2</m></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>f'''($a) = </m></cell>
                  <cell><answer name="f2a">$$fPPP($a)</answer></cell>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>T_3'''($a) = </m></cell>
                  <cell><m>6c_3</m></cell>
                </row>
              </tabular>

    </li>
    <li>
        <p>
          Now, set <m>T_3(0) = f(0)</m>.  This means that <m>c_0 = </m><answer name="c0Entered">$b0</answer>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Next, set <m>T_3'(0) = f'(0)</m>.  This means that <m>c_1 = </m><answer name="c1Entered">$b1</answer>.
        </p>
        <p>
        Then, set <m>T_3''(0) = f''(0)</m>.  This means that <m>c_2=</m><answer name="c2Entered">$b2</answer>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Finally, set <m>T_3'''(0) = f'''(0)</m>. This means that <m>c_3=</m><answer name="c3Entered">$b3</answer>
        </p>
      <p>
        Putting it all together, what is the resulting formula for <m>T_3(x)</m>?
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>T_3(x) = </m><mathInput name="T3Entered" minWidth="100"/><answer><award><when>$T3Entered=$T3</when></award></answer>
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
          Recall that the tangent line approximation <m>T_1</m> to <m>f(x) = \sin(x)</m> at <m>a = 0</m> is <m>T_1(x) = x</m>.  Use appropriate computing technology to plot the cubic approximation <m>T_3(x)</m> you found in (b) along with <m>f(x)</m> and <m>T_1(x)</m> in the same window shown in the following figure.
      </p>
      <graph size="medium" xMin="-2" xMax="2" yMin="-2" yMax="2" showNavigation="false" fixAxes="true">
        $f
        $T1
        <label anchor="(1,1.5)"><m>T_1(x) = x</m></label>
        <label anchor="(1.2,0.5)"><m>f(x) = \sin(x)</m></label>
      </graph>
      <p>
        What do you observe about the approximation of <m>f(x)</m> by <m>T_3(x)</m> compared to the approximation of <m>f(x)</m> by <m>T_1(x)</m>?  Complete the following sentence to explain.
      </p>
      <p>
        The approximation of <m>f(x)</m> by <m>T_3(x)</m> is <choiceInput inline name="ans1">
          <choice>?</choice>
          <choice>worse than</choice>
          <choice>about the same as</choice>
          <choice>better than</choice>
        </choiceInput> the approximation by <m>T_1(x)</m> and this comparison holds on <choiceInput inline name="ans2">
          <choice>?</choice>
          <choice>a smaller interval</choice>
          <choice>about the same interval as</choice>
          <choice>a wider interval than</choice>
        </choiceInput> where <m>T_1(x)</m> provides an accurate approximation.  For example, <m>|f(1) - T_1(1)| \approx</m><mathInput name="ans3"/> while <m>|f(1) - T_3(1)| \approx </m><mathInput name="ans4"/> (enter your numerical results to at least an accuracy of <m>0.0001</m>).
      </p>
      <p>
        <answer allowedErrorInNumbers="0.0001" allowedErrorIsAbsolute><award><when>$ans1.selectedIndices=4 and $ans2.selectedIndices=4 and $ans3=abs($$f(1)-$$T1(1)) and $ans4=abs($$f(1)-$$T3(1)) </when></award></answer>
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        In our work so far, we've seen that raising the degree of polynomial improves the approximation of the original function <m>f(x)</m>.  If we wanted to find a degree <m>9</m> approximation to <m>f(x) = \sin(x)</m>, we'd need to find a polynomial whose value and first <m>9</m> derivatives match those of the sine function at <m>a = 0</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Fill in the blanks below to determine the next six derivatives of <m>f(x) = \sin(x)</m>, up to <m>f^{(9)}(x)</m>.

      </p>
              <tabular>
                <row>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>f(x) = </m></cell>
                  <cell><m>$f</m></cell>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>f^{(5)}(x) = </m></cell>
                  <cell><answer>$fP</answer></cell>
                </row>
               <row>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>f'(x) = </m></cell>
                  <cell><m>$fP</m></cell>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>f^{(6)}(x) = </m></cell>
                  <cell><answer>$fPP</answer></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>f''(x) = </m></cell>
                  <cell><m>$fPP</m></cell>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>f^{(7)}(x) = </m></cell>
                  <cell><answer>$fPPP</answer></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>f'''(x) = </m></cell>
                  <cell><m>$fPPP</m></cell>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>f^{(8)}(x) = </m></cell>
                  <cell><answer>$f</answer></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>f^{(4)}(x) = </m></cell>
                  <cell><answer>$f</answer></cell>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>f^{(9)}(x) = </m></cell>
                  <cell><answer>$fP</answer></cell>
                </row>
              </tabular>
       <p>
         We notice from this collection of derivatives that if we start with any <m>n</m>th derivative of <m>f(x) = \sin(x)</m> and take <answer><choiceInput inline>
           <choice>?</choice>
           <choice>one</choice>
           <choice>two</choice>
           <choice>three</choice>
           <choice credit="1">four</choice>
           <choice>five</choice>
         </choiceInput></answer>
         more derivatives, we get the same function as we started with.
       </p>
      <p>
         We also notice that when <m>n</m> is even, the <m>n</m>th derivative of <m>f</m> at <m>0</m>, denoted <m>f^{(n)}(0)</m>, is <choiceInput inline name="neven">
           <choice>?</choice>
           <choice><m>0</m></choice>
           <choice><m>1</m></choice>
           <choice><m>-1</m></choice>
           <choice>either <m>1</m> or <m>-1</m></choice>
         </choiceInput> because the even derivatives are always <choiceInput inline name="neven2">
           <choice>?</choice>
           <choice><m>\sin(x)</m> or <m>-\sin(x)</m></choice>
           <choice><m>\cos(x)</m> or <m>-\cos(x)</m></choice>
         </choiceInput>,
        and when <m>n</m> is odd, <m>f^{(n)}(0)</m> is <choiceInput inline name="nodd">
           <choice>?</choice>
           <choice><m>0</m></choice>
           <choice><m>1</m></choice>
           <choice><m>-1</m></choice>
           <choice>either <m>1</m> or <m>-1</m></choice>
         </choiceInput> because the odd derivatives are always <choiceInput inline name="nodd2">
           <choice>?</choice>
           <choice><m>\sin(x)</m> or <m>-\sin(x)</m></choice>
           <choice><m>\cos(x)</m> or <m>-\cos(x)</m></choice>
         </choiceInput>,
       </p>
      <answer><award><when>$neven.selectedIndices=2 and $neven2.selectedIndices=2 and $nodd.selectedIndices=5 and $nodd2.selectedIndices=3 </when></award></answer>
    </li>
  </ol>
</p>